I can't point you to any particular online source, but I think I can
sum up the basic points of "thee, thou, durst" in brief.  :)

   "Thou" is the archaic pronoun for "you" (singular, informal),
similar to "tu" in Spanish or "du" in German.  Incidentally, the Quakers
(a religious sect) traditionally refer to everyone as "thou," as a
kind of egalitarian gesture.  (Why they can't just refer to everyone
as "you" instead, I've never understood... ;)

   "Thee" is the objective form of "thou": "What hast thou done
to him?  What has he done to thee?"
   "Thy" is the possessive form of "thou": "Do unto thy neighbor..."

   "Ye" is the archaic pronoun for "you" (plural, either formal
or informal), similar to "ustedes" in some regional dialects of
Spanish (and probably even *more* similar to something else).
"Ye" is the objective form of "you."  "Friends, Romans, countrymen,
lend me your [not "thy"] ears."
   N.B. that "thou/thee" is "backwards" compared to "he/him" in
terms of which form ends in "-ee."  That's just the way it is.

   "Thy" and "thine", "my" and "mine": Essentially, use whatever
feels right.  The basic distinction is similar to the "a"/"an"
distinction: "a banana, my banana"/"an apple, mine apple."
   Except of course that "thine" also works like "mine" in talking
about objects:

   "That's my book!  It's mine!"
   "That's thy book?  It's thine?"
   "Yeah, that's what I said."


Besides that, it's just knowing how to conjugate common verbs for
the archaic "thou":

   I have, thou hast, he hath, ye have...
   I say, thou sayest, he saith, ye say...
   I dare, thou durst, he dareth (or "durth"), ye dare...
   I am, thou art, he is (or "be"), ye be...
   I do, thou dost, he doth, ye do...


And then past tense and future tense:

   Where wert thou last night?
   Where wilt thou be tomorrow morning?

And that's it!  Simple, really!

